# STEWARD environment. Copy to `.env` and fill in — Bun loads it automatically.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored and must stay that way: it holds credentials.
# Every value below is optional; STEWARD runs with none of them set.

# ---- server ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Port to listen on. Default 3000.
PORT=3211

# "production" turns off dev-only behaviour (stack traces in error responses,
# warnings about unresolved component bindings). Default: development.
# NODE_ENV=production


# ---- Google Drive (optional) -----------------------------------------------
# Without these, documents are stored on local disk and everything still works.
#
# THIS FILE IS A DEVELOPMENT CONVENIENCE ONLY (0017). A released binary carries no
# Google credentials at all and reads them from Settings > Google credentials, which
# is also where anyone running a download puts them. Values here still win over an
# empty Settings, so a checkout behaves as it always has — but nothing in this file
# reaches a build, and no CI secret supplies it.
#
# Setup, once, at https://console.cloud.google.com :
#   1. Create a project.
#   2. APIs & Services > Library: enable "Google Drive API"
#      (and "Google Sheets API" if you want the sheet mirror later).
#   3. Google Auth Platform > Branding: app name + support emails.
#   4. Data Access: add the scope .../auth/drive.file — it is NON-SENSITIVE,
#      so no verification review is required.
#   5. Audience: PUBLISH THE APP TO PRODUCTION. While it sits in "Testing",
#      Google expires refresh tokens after 7 days and you must reconnect weekly.
#   6. Credentials > Create credentials > OAuth client ID
#      > Application type: DESKTOP APP. Copy the id and secret below.
#
# Desktop clients allow loopback redirects on any port, so there is no redirect
# URI to register. This build listens on:
#   http://127.0.0.1:${PORT}/oauth/google/callback
#
# The "secret" of a desktop OAuth client is not truly confidential — Google says
# so, and PKCE is what actually protects the exchange. It still does not belong
# in git.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Folder created in the connected account's own Drive to hold STEWARD's files.
# GOOGLE_DRIVE_FOLDER=STEWARD

# ---- Google Picker (optional, for linking EXISTING Drive files) ------------
# The `drive.file` scope deliberately cannot see files STEWARD did not create,
# so linking one means the operator hands it over through Google's own Picker.
# That needs two extra values from the SAME Cloud project:
#   1. APIs & Services > Library: enable "Google Picker API".
#   2. Credentials > Create credentials > API key. Restrict it to the Picker
#      API. It is a browser key — it identifies the project, it grants nothing.
#   3. The project NUMBER (not the id): Cloud Console home, "Project number".
#      Picker sends it as the app id, which is how Drive knows which app to
#      grant per-file access to when a file is picked.
# Without these, everything else still works — the "Link from Drive" button
# just says what is missing instead of opening.
# GOOGLE_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_PROJECT_NUMBER=


# ---- AI chat (optional) ----------------------------------------------------
# The assistant panel talks to a local Ollama daemon. Needs `ollama serve` and
# the model pulled; without it the panel reports that it cannot reach the model.
# OLLAMA_URL=http://localhost:11434
# OLLAMA_MODEL=llama3.2:3b


# ---- PDF rendering (optional) ----------------------------------------------
# Ticket PDFs print through a headless Chrome/Chromium that STEWARD launches.
# Common install paths are probed automatically; set this if yours is elsewhere.
# CHROME_PATH=/Applications/Google Chrome.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome


# ---- database (optional) ---------------------------------------------------
# Defaults to data/steward.db. DEMO=1 switches to data/demo.db instead, so
# seeding fictional data never touches real records.
# STEWARD_DB=data/steward.db
# DEMO=1
